# What every OpenSigner image needs to come up and be used, whatever it
# runs on. A driver that quietly loses a dependency — because the
# subsystem above it went out — fails here rather than on the owner's desk.

BLK_DEV_INITRD       the whole root filesystem is linked into the kernel image
DEVTMPFS_MOUNT       the kernel makes /dev itself; nothing in the image could
FB                   the interface the shell writes every frame through
VT                   the framebuffer console owns the display until the app's first frame, and it needs a virtual terminal
FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE  what paints the boot logo, so a black display means the firmware and not a silent working device
INPUT_EVDEV          /dev/input/eventN, which is how the shell reads touches, keys and pointers
VIDEO_DEV            /dev/video0, the V4L2 node the scanner opens
USB_VIDEO_CLASS      a USB webcam is a camera every one of these devices supports
VFAT_FS              the exchange partition, where a PSBT arrives and a signed one goes back

# Hardening (PLANNING.md 16.140). The options only one architecture has
# are in that board's list.
INIT_ON_ALLOC_DEFAULT_ON              every page and slab object is zeroed when it is handed out
INIT_ON_FREE_DEFAULT_ON               and when it is freed, so a kernel copy of a secret does not outlive its use
HARDENED_USERCOPY                     copies between the kernel and a process are checked against the object they come from
FORTIFY_SOURCE                        the kernel's memory and string functions are checked against the sizes the compiler knows
SLAB_FREELIST_HARDENED                a corrupted slab free list is detected
SLAB_FREELIST_RANDOM                  where a slab object lands is not predictable
SHUFFLE_PAGE_ALLOCATOR                nor where a page lands
BUG_ON_DATA_CORRUPTION                detected corruption stops the kernel instead of being stepped over
SCHED_STACK_END_CHECK                 so does an overrun kernel stack
PANIC_ON_OOPS                         a kernel that has oopsed is in a state nobody chose; on a signer, stopping is the safe result
ZERO_CALL_USED_REGS                   registers are cleared on return, leaving fewer gadgets for a return-oriented chain
SECURITY_DMESG_RESTRICT               the kernel log, with its addresses, is for root only
SECURITY_YAMA                         rcS sets ptrace_scope to 3: no process traces another
SECURITY_LOCKDOWN_LSM                 one switch for every interface that reads or writes kernel memory or raw hardware
SECURITY_LOCKDOWN_LSM_EARLY           on before anything else starts
LOCK_DOWN_KERNEL_FORCE_CONFIDENTIALITY at its strictest level, and not one a process can lower
